.\" perror.3 - print error message
.TH PERROR 3 "2026-09-29" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
perror \- print error message to standard error
.SH LIBRARY
.B libc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.BI "void perror(const char *" s ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR perror ()
function writes a message describing the current value of
.I errno
to the standard error stream
.RB ( stderr ).
.PP
If
.I s
is non-NULL and points to a non-empty string, the output is
.I s
followed by a colon, a space, the message
.BR strerror (3)
returns for
.IR errno ,
and a newline:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
/nonexistent: No such file or directory
.EE
.RE
.PP
Otherwise (if
.I s
is NULL or points to an empty string), only the message and a newline
are written.
.PP
The value of
.I errno
is read when
.BR perror ()
is called, so it should be called before any other library call can
change
.IR errno .
.BR perror ()
leaves
.I errno
unchanged.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR perror ()
returns no value.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR strerror (3),
.BR fprintf (3),
.BR errno (3)
.SH STANDARDS
ISO C99, POSIX.1-2024.
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program reports a failed open using
.BR perror ().
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    FILE *fp = fopen("/nonexistent", "r");

    if (fp == NULL) {
        perror("/nonexistent");
        return 1;
    }
    fclose(fp);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
It prints:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
/nonexistent: No such file or directory
.EE
.RE
